      <data key="d5">1788522070</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous cell carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Squamous cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell carcinoma is a diverse group of cancers that can affect various parts of the body, including the skin, lungs, and cervix. It primarily originates from squamous cells, which are flat, thin cells that line surfaces such as the skin and internal organs. This type of cancer is particularly prevalent in the skin, where it can develop from squamous cells that line the epidermis. In the lungs, squamous cell carcinoma is a subtype of non-small cell lung cancer, forming from cells that line the bronchi. Additionally, squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of cervical cancer, characterized by the presence of squamous cells in the cervical tissue. The term "squamous cell carcinoma" encompasses cancers that arise in squamous cells, which are found in the tissues that form the surface of the skin and in other areas of the body, such as the mouth, throat, and anus. These cancers are often aggressive and can spread to other parts of the body if left untreated.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-133&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-138&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-140&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-141&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-185&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-189</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788522076</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A shortened chromosome 22 resulting from a genetic rearrangement in chronic myeloid leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A new, abnormal chromosome resulting from a genetic rearrangement in Chronic Myelogenous Leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A shortened chromosome 22 resulting from a break-off and trade place of a piece of chromosome 9 and a piece of chromosome 22, causing chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788522076</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A fused gene resulting from a genetic rearrangement in chronic myeloid leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific abnormal gene associated with Chronic Myelogenous Leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A fused gene created when a piece of chromosome 9 and a piece of chromosome 22 break off and trade places, causing chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).&lt;SEP&gt;A specific abnormal gene that is created when a piece of chromosome 9 and a piece of chromosome 22 break off and trade places, resulting in a fused gene and a shortened chromosome 22 called the Philadelphia (Ph) chromosome.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-074&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1788522076</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia (Ph) chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia (Ph) chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A shortened chromosome 22 resulting from a genetic rearrangement in chronic myeloid leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A shortened chromosome 22 that contains a small part of chromosome 9, which is the hallmark of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML).</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3d7a4f05339f0e3f3870b407d21d9c8a-chunk-075</data>
